{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/991083-wod-blood-and-silk-s-naming-and-transliteration-sidebar","id":991083,"title":"WoD: Blood and Silk's 'Naming and Transliteration\" Sidebar","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-10-21T13:22:21-07:00","id":991083,"title":"WoD: Blood and Silk's 'Naming and Transliteration\" Sidebar","content":"I've griped about this old goof on page 8 of its book an awful lot. Let me break down the reasons why. As the sidebar reads...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>WoD: Blood &amp; Silk</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Naming and Transliteration<br /> There are two dominant styles of transliteration in from Chinese characters into English words, the Yale transliteration and Pinyin.</div> </div> </div> </div>This would have been a terrible error even at the time, because people should have known about at least Wade-Giles, which is the one with all of the weird aspiration marks everywhere and more umlauts than (Hanyu) Pinyin, and which shows up everywhere, often poorly, in KotE material, as well as its simplified form, which uses hs instead of aspiration marks and was used as the Romanization style for Chinese in the original Stargazers Tribebook. Less well known was Legge, but it was still a rather old system that I'm guessing probably should have shown up if you were reading about Romanization of Chinese. Those, of course, are all for Mandarin, aka the Common Language or National Language. Cantonese also uses a system of Yale and its own Pinyin, as well as systems like Meyer-Wempe (like Wade-Giles for Cantonese!), a missionary-led system originally meant for proselytizing Christianity, and Jyutping (the jy is pronounced y); Jyutping, along with Cantonese Pinyin, are the official Cantonese transcription styles of Hong Kong.<br /> <br /> Yale wasn't even quite a dominant system as such; it was made by the university of the same name to get American soldiers to correctly pronounce Chinese while they were in action overseas, back in 1943. Afterward, Wade-Giles was still more commonly used.<br /> <br /> The text does correctly identify that the PRC uses Pinyin (they came up with it), but advises checking reference materials if Romanizations don't quite match up. This would be admirable, but for the following mistake:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> ... check to see if your reference material refers to the modern capital of China as Peking (Yale) or as Beijing (Pinyin) </div> </div> </div>Oh, dear. No, Peking isn't Yale; the Yale for Beijing is actually the same as the Pinyin. Peking derives from the extremely archaic Legge Romanization (which was replaced by Wade-Giles). You may wonder how the hell you get King and Jing being the same thing, but it's actually about a sound shift in Mandarin that took place in the 19th century, when a lot of k sounds became what modern Pinyin would place as j or q sounds. That, incidentally, is also why some Zhong Lung titles look weird, as they were directly taken from an old Wade-Giles Romanized dictionary. You can even find the very same excerpt on Wikipedia's entry on chinese dragons. Also, Yale didn't even exist until well into the 20th century, so it couldn't have been around to have been incorporated into archaic texts.<br /> <br /> Moving on to the next pertinent quote, regarding use of names familiar to readers who aren't directly familiar with Chinese:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> More specifically, we use Confucius and Genghis Khan, rather than the more technically correct Kung Fuxi and Chinggis (or Qinggis) Khan. </div> </div> </div>The Romanization of Confucius that they listed was a bit off in a couple of ways. See, while the above asserted that this text would be using Pinyin, and indeed x is only used in Hanyu Pinyin, Kung for Kong is a distinct Wade-Giles-ism (the sound that would seem a bit like the English word cone). Also, In Hanyu Pinyin, that part of Confucius is actually Fuzi, not Fuxi; the former's second syllable sounds like a drawn out ts sound, while the latter's second syllable is somewhere between an sy and an sh, and its vowel is actually pronounced, instead of just extending the consonant as part of the empty rime that marks some Mandarin initial and final combinations (such as Shi, which sounds kind of like the English word sure, but is faster to get to the r part). So, already, in the very same sidebar where they claimed they used Pinyin throughout the text, a goof has happened, to say nothing of the glossary on the column to the left using hsien instead of xian, among other uses of W-G.<br /> <br /> As for the Great Khan, There's a bit of an error. Mongolian doesn't use use the highly-aspirated ch for his name (which is the Mandarin q, or the Simplified Wade chh), so the Qinggis form is right out. Also, there's apparently a double a that's supposed to go there, as in Khaan instead of Khan.<br /> <br /> Thankfully, Onyx Path does a lot better with other languages, so I consider expecting never to have to see the likes of Chi'iu Muh, Chu-ih-yo, or chi'n ta again reasonable, even if Chi'ih Ming managed to make it into the Zhong Lung writeup (as an extra added on via a KS stretch goal, the beast courts section didn't have much time to get edited, I think). But damn, this sidebar. If you were to take it to heart, you'd be worse off than someone who knew nothing, thanks to the errors it made in forgetting about Wade-Giles of all things, or its Time-Travelling Yale error, thinking that q is universally similar to ch in all Romanizations you encounter... I just don't know what happened, here. The sidebars in Rage Across the World and Land of Eight Million Dreams actually had correct information in them, even with a few errors here and there. Perhaps my perspective is also skewed by the fact that Geoff Grabowski worked on the book, and at a later date, as developer of Exalted, gave Jenna Moran specific Romanization instructions regarding the references to T'ai-chi in Prismatic Arrangement of Creation style. I... did not expect something like what I read in WoD: B&amp;S.<br /> <br /> ...rather unfortunate abbreviation that book has.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-08T05:43:49-08:00","id":1000234,"title":"","content":"Just so you know, while there are little replies to your ruminations on the chinese transcription, I've always found them quite illuminating!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-08T10:55:20-08:00","id":1000333,"title":"","content":"I started with pinyin and now everything else just confuses me. A lot of people get hung up on C being a &quot;Ts&quot; sound, but honestly we do something pretty similar with it English every time we use a soft C (centennial, Caesar, etc.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2507-caitiff-primogen","name":"Caitiff Primogen","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2507_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}